<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pyu city-states</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 1050 CE (approx.) · capital Sri Ksetra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A network of walled brick cities in the dry zone of Myanmar that brought Buddhism, writing and urban life to the Irrawaddy valley for over a thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From about the 2nd century BCE the Pyu, a Tibeto-Burman-speaking people, built large walled cities in the dry zone of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar</a>, among them <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beikthano">Beikthano</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/halin">Halin</a>, Maingmaw and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-ksetra-kingdom">Sri Ksetra</a>. Their growth was tied to irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming and trade routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each city was ruled by its own dynasty, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-ksetra-kingdom">Sri Ksetra</a> became the leading centre by the 5th century. The Pyu wrote their own language in a script derived from Brahmi, and Chinese sources of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> period describe a gentle Buddhist people with many monasteries, music and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coinage. Cylindrical stupas, urn burials and gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> leaves survive from their sites.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Raids by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanzhao">Nanzhao</a> in the 830s, including the sack of a Pyu city, probably <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/halin">Halin</a>, in 832, broke Pyu power, and the cities declined as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> plain and built the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom</a>. Pyu culture was absorbed into Burman society; three Pyu ancient cities became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2014.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pyu-city-states.jpg" alt="Pyu city-states" width="320" height="641"><figcaption>Image: Kanguole · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyu_city-states_physical_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 1050 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sri Ksetra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pyu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pyu, Tircul</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=425&amp;focus=pyu-city-states" aria-label="Pyu city-states on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7245 1348 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pyu city-states lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7565 1604L7550 1651M7565 1604L7573 1693M7565 1604L7565 1604M7565 1604L7580 1736"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7550 1651h0M7573 1693h0M7565 1604h0M7580 1736h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7565 1604h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7565 1604h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=425&amp;focus=pyu-city-states">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
